      "writtenOn": "2021-10-19T13:40:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I suppose the fact that I think about this differently from both you and SkRect is an argument to let the caller choose, but here\u0027s how I\u0027ve thought Rect contains/intersect should behave:\n\nA Rect represents a closed set of points, so any point on its edge is still part of the edge. A point query is equivalent to Rect::Point(p) where it\u0027s width and height are 0 (not different from your model here). However, if Rect is considered a closed set, then both contains(pt) and intersect(pt) should return true for points on the edges.\n\nConversely, if we consider Rect to be an open set, then points on the edge to not belong to the Rect and both contains(pt) and intersect(pt) should return false for points on the edges.\n\nI don\u0027t get the reason for intersect(pt) to return false from a theoretic standpoint, unless it\u0027s just there to allow the user to choose?\n\n\nGoing back to SkRect\u0027s behavior, it\u0027s contains(pt) seems to be motivated by SkIRect\u0027s contains implementation. But SkIRect explicitly states that an integer point is equivalent to a 1x1 SkIRect, so SkIRect not containing points on the right/bottom edge makes sense. For SkRect where a point is 0x0, that logic doesn\u0027t transfer as well. However, SkRect also returns false for contains/intersect if either SkRect is empty. This means that a point as a 0x0 rect can never be contained in anything, even if it\u0027s ltrb values are inside the other SkRect. So I agree that SkRect tries to give you the wrong answer 😜",
      "parentUuid": "1ce26795_ae8adc11",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 95,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 95,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "f7552afdd47dd81e65636bdb9dbb10af50c7e422",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
